t play-action rate in the league.","offense":"The Bears offense has been well above average by EPA per play so far. They have thrown more than almost anyone relative to the situation, even when the score has not called for it. Their biggest outliers in how they line up are the highest play-action rate in the league. Caleb Williams has run the passing game, with Luther Burden III leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Bears have one of the best defenses in the league this season, ranking 8th or better in three of the five main efficiency measures. The one weak spot has been explosive play rate allowed, where they rank 21st. Montez Sweat has been as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise is Kyler Gordon, who has 4 sacks despite playing only 56% of the snaps. Their scheme has been fairly conventional, and the closest thing to a signature is a number of defenders in the box that ranks 4th highest in the league.","special":"The Bears have been about average in how often they go for it on fourth down. Their kickers have made 79% of field goals, which is well below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into tight ends, about 8% of the money on the books.","players":"Caleb Williams has played nearly every snap at quarterback and thrown for 1,546 yards. Luther Burden III has been the clear top target with 23% of the looks and 644 receiving yards. D'Andre Swift has handled most of the work in the backfield with 60% of the carries. On defense, Austin Booker leads the team with 5 sacks.","coaching":"Ben Johnson is calling the offense as head coach and Dennis Allen is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2025 Vikings under Kevin O'Connell and the 2022 Lions under Ben Johnson. Their defense looks most like the 2023 Cowboys under Dan Quinn and the 2024 Jets under Jeff Ulbrich.","trends":"The offense went from 8th in EPA per play in 2025 to 10th so far this season, about where it was. The defense moved from 19th in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 8th so far this season, a big step forward. The biggest change in approach was two point attempt rate, which went from 12% to 0%.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}